(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-data-validity-variation-quiz"]={"title":"Data, Validity and Variation Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why does the level of measurement matter in medical research?","choices":["It determines whether the study is primary or secondary","It determines the prevalence of the condition","It determines what can legitimately be said about two values","It determines whether the measurement is reliable"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: nominal categories can only be counted, ordinal values can be ranked, and only interval data supports statements about how much two values differ. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which question tests the content validity of a depression screening scale?","choices":["Are the items representative of accepted symptoms of depression?","Does a high scorer have the affect of a depressed person?","Would a clinician interviewing the patient reach the same conclusion?","Does the scale give the same score on a second administration?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the sample items make it concrete: sadness lasting more than two weeks, irritability and difficulty sleeping. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the mean?","choices":["The sum of all values divided by the number of observations","The middle score, with half above and half below","The most frequently occurring value","The difference between the highest and lowest values"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is described as the best estimate of central tendency, with the caveat that extreme outliers can pull it away from the bulk of the data. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What defines nominal data?","choices":["Categories with order but undefined spacing","Categories without any specified order","Values with order and equal defined intervals","Values that can take any point on a continuum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with blood group and eye color as examples. Nothing about the categories implies that one is more or less than another. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Why is there no clear-cut point where disease begins?","choices":["Diagnostic tests are insufficiently precise","Disease is often acquired by degrees","Reference ranges have not yet been established","Most clinical data are dichotomous"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the progression is laid out as a sequence: susceptibility, then pre-symptomatic disease, then clinical disease, then dysfunction, then death. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What is random measurement error due to?","choices":["A flaw in the measurement process","Genuine biologic change representing disease","Chance, with equal probability of being above or below","Differences between the patients being compared"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it manifests as a lack of reliability. Systematic measurement error, by contrast, is a flaw in the process and shows as a lack of validity. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A patient's blood pressure differs between two readings taken at different times of day. Which variation does that illustrate?","choices":["Systematic variation, since the condition is known to affect it","True biologic variation from unknown random factors","Interobserver variation","Random measurement error"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: time of day is a known condition of measurement affecting the value, alongside position, smoking beforehand and the white coat phenomenon. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What defines systematic variation in clinical measurement?","choices":["Known conditions of measurement affect the values obtained","Unknown factors each contributing a small random effect","Two observers reading the same finding differently","The measurement instrument being read imprecisely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with blood pressure varying by time of day, after a cigarette, by patient position, or through the white coat phenomenon. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What does the example of a sickle cell trait carrier illustrate?","choices":["A normal finding can coexist with disease","Statistical and clinical abnormality always coincide","Extreme values are always undesirable","An abnormal finding can exist without the person being sick"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is one half of a pair: the other is that a normal test does not necessarily rule disease out. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What does a high-normal value still tell you?","choices":["Nothing, since it lies within the reference range","Risk is higher than at the low-normal end","That disease is present but not yet detectable","That the measurement should be repeated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so a value inside the reference range is not uniformly reassuring: where it sits within the range still carries information. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which kind of measurement error shows itself as a lack of validity?","choices":["Random error","Intrasubject variation","Interobserver variation","Systematic error"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because a flaw in the process pushes every reading the same way, so the measure is consistently not measuring what it should. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"How can the effect of random variation on a measurement be reduced?","choices":["By using a different observer","By calibrating the instrument","By taking an average","By narrowing the reference range"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because random departures fall on both sides of the truth with equal likelihood, so they cancel as observations accumulate. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which three types of data does measurement produce?","choices":["Continuous, discrete and dichotomous","Qualitative, quantitative and mixed","Nominal, ordinal and interval","Primary, secondary and tertiary"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they form a ladder: nominal has categories without order, ordinal adds order without defined spacing, and interval adds equal defined spacing. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which examples show that extreme values may be desirable?","choices":["The higher systolic pressure and lower cholesterol ranges","The middle of every reference range","Any value two standard deviations from the mean","The lower systolic pressure range and the higher cholesterol range"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, referring to the lower end of the systolic blood pressure range and the higher end of the high-density lipoprotein range. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"When should intervention be considered in the face of a changed value?","choices":["Whenever the value falls outside the reference range","Whenever the value differs from the previous one","Only when there is a true pathological change","Whenever two observers disagree about the value"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why the source of the variation has to be identified: acting on biologic variation or measurement error means treating something that is not there. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which pair are examples of continuous data?","choices":["Number of children and number of heart attacks","Blood group and eye color","Cancer stage and pitting edema grade","Blood pressure and weight"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because each can take any value in a range, bounded only by how precisely the measurement can be made. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What caution accompanies the discussion of defining abnormality?","choices":["Always treat values outside the range","Never repeat an abnormal test","Be careful of labeling","Disregard values within the reference range"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because cutoffs are often arbitrary and a statistically abnormal value need not mean the patient is unwell. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Can a measurement be reliable without being valid?","choices":["No, reliability guarantees validity","Yes, it can be consistently wrong","No, the two terms mean the same thing","Yes, but only when the sample is small"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and that combination of low validity with high reliability is the one to watch for, since repeated agreement looks like confirmation. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What does statistical abnormality not necessarily imply?","choices":["That the value was measured accurately","That the value lies outside the reference range","Practical or clinical abnormality","That the test was performed correctly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with a serum cholesterol of 201 as the example: above the desired level, but not clinically significant in itself. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What may inter-patient variation be due to?","choices":["Only inherent biological differences","Only differences in how the measurement was taken","Only errors made by the observer","Biological differences, measurement conditions, and measurement error"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it matters because answers to clinical questions about an individual are most often derived from groups of patients with similar conditions. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which is an example of ordinal data?","choices":["Blood group","Cancer staging from I to IV","Blood pressure","Number of heart attacks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside pitting edema graded one to four plus, patient status or condition, and the drug risk categories for birth defects. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does a normal test result not do?","choices":["Rule out disease","Reassure the patient","Fall within the reference range","Require confirmation by repetition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which pairs with the converse: an abnormal finding does not establish that the person is sick, as with a sickle cell trait carrier. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What can be said about two ordinal values?","choices":["By exactly how much one exceeds the other","Whether one is more, less or equally desirable","Nothing beyond that they differ","That the difference is the same at every step"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and what cannot be said is by how much, because the intervals between adjacent categories are not defined or equal. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is said about cutoff points for normal and abnormal?","choices":["They are derived from measured healthy populations","They are fixed by national agencies","They mark the point where disease begins","They are often arbitrary"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the warning attached follows from it: be careful of labeling, since a label applied on an arbitrary threshold follows the patient afterwards. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"By what other names is the normal distribution known?","choices":["The Gaussian distribution or bell curve","The frequency distribution","The interquartile distribution","The skewed distribution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and all three names describe the same symmetric shape in which mean, median and mode coincide. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is construct validity?","choices":["The measure relates to others believed part of the same phenomenon","The measure predicts an observable event","The measure includes every relevant dimension","The measure produces the same result each time"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, as in whether a clinician interviewing a patient for depression reaches the same determination as a screening test does. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"By what two things is a frequency distribution described?","choices":["Validity and reliability","Central tendency and dispersion","Incidence and prevalence","Bias and chance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: central tendency locates the middle of the distribution, and dispersion describes how widely the values are spread around it. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What is the net effect of random variation across many measurements?","choices":["It biases the result in one direction","It makes the measurement invalid","It cannot be estimated or reduced","It balances out, so the true state is not misrepresented"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, although individual measurements can still mislead, which is why averaging reduces it and statistical analysis can estimate it. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is the median?","choices":["The middle score, with half the values on each side","The most frequently occurring value","The average of all the values","The range between the 25th and 75th percentiles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and because it depends on position rather than magnitude, an extreme value at one end moves it far less than it moves the mean. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What proportion of values falls within one standard deviation of the mean in a normal distribution?","choices":["About 95 percent","About two thirds","About half","Essentially all of them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and 95 percent fall within two standard deviations, which is where the conventional reference range comes from. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 73"}]};
